    "q-130772": {
      "dom_id": "step-q130772",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Which of the following are elementary matrices?\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,S=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "An elementary matrix is a matrix obtained from the identity matrix by applying exactly one elementary row operation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- The matrices $P$ and $Q$ are not elementary matrices because they can't be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying only one elementary row operation.\n\n- The matrix $S$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =S\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $S$ is an elementary matrix.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
